<Base title="Why Code-First · The Counter-Argument to n8n, Make & Zapier · Pravine Mani" description="Why I refuse no-code automation rebuilds. The math: $0/mo platform vs $300/mo glue. Plus version control. Observability. No vendor lock-in." jsonLd={jsonLd}>

<section class="page-hero">
  <div class="breadcrumb"><span class="gr">$</span> cat ~/why-code-first.md <span class="you">→ the counter-argument</span></div>
  <h1>Why code-first.<br><span class="accent">The argument against n8n, Make, and Zapier.</span></h1>
  <p class="lead">A common question on scoping calls is whether the workflow can be built in n8n, Make, or Zapier instead of as code. For production work, the answer is no. This page documents the reasoning, with cost figures, architectural trade-offs, and recommendations for when the no-code option is actually the right call.</p>
</section>

<section class="section manifesto">

  <div class="manifesto-body">

    <h2>The cost math.</h2>
    <p>Consider a workflow that runs 100 times a day — a daily operations brief, a lead-routing pipeline, an AI-assisted inbox triage. The comparison below uses representative figures from a recent client engagement.</p>

    <div class="compare">
      <div class="compare-col">
        <div class="ctop"><span class="lab">CODE-FIRST</span><span class="num">$73 / yr</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>Vercel Hobby: $0/mo</li>
          <li>Vercel cron + serverless: free under 100K invocations</li>
          <li>Supabase free tier: covers it</li>
          <li>Resend free tier: 3K emails/mo</li>
          <li>Anthropic API at ~$0.20/day: $73/year</li>
          <li>Domain: pre-existing</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-col compare-bad">
        <div class="ctop"><span class="lab">NO-CODE STACK</span><span class="num">$720 / yr</span></div>
        <ul>
          <li>n8n Cloud Starter: $20/mo</li>
          <li>Make Core: $9/mo</li>
          <li>Per-action quotas hit at ~5K events/mo</li>
          <li>Premium tier needed at scale</li>
          <li>Each new workflow = new subscription</li>
          <li>$0 of it is yours when you stop paying</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>The cost difference is the more visible factor. <strong>The architectural dependency is the more significant one</strong>, and it compounds over time.</p>

    <h2>The four reasons.</h2>

    <h3>01 · Code is owned. Subscriptions are rented.</h3>
    <p>If the n8n subscription lapses, the workflow stops functioning. The logic does not exist outside the platform's interface, the credentials are stored in the platform's vault, and the pricing terms are subject to change without notice. A code-first equivalent runs on infrastructure the client already owns: the source lives in the project repository, the deployment runs on the client's Vercel account, and the workflow continues to operate independent of any consulting relationship.</p>

    <h3>02 · Versioned in git. Reviewable. Revertible.</h3>
    <p>Every change to a code-first workflow produces a commit with a diff, an author, a timestamp, a CI run, and a deploy preview. Reverting a problematic change takes a single command. Equivalent changes in a visual workflow platform happen inside a UI on the vendor's server, with no source-control history, no peer-review surface, and no rollback path unless someone exported the workflow JSON before the change was made.</p>

    <h3>03 · Observable. Debuggable in production.</h3>
    <p>When a workflow fails in production, code-first deployments produce structured Vercel logs, Sentry traces, and analytics events through a stack the client's engineering team already understands. Visual workflow platforms typically surface failures as a generic "node failed" message in their UI, without a stack trace, the failing API response body, or a way to replay the event locally. Debugging time for the same class of error is meaningfully different across the two approaches.</p>

    <h3>04 · The integration library is a feature, not a moat.</h3>
    <p>Visual workflow platforms market large integration catalogues — 500-plus connectors in some cases. In practice, most client engagements rely on three to five of those integrations. The remaining integrations contribute to the platform's pricing without contributing to the workflow's function. For the three to five that are actually needed, the equivalent code is typically short: an Anthropic SDK call is approximately 12 lines, a Stripe webhook handler is around 60 lines, a Supabase query is a single line.</p>

    <h2>When the no-code option is appropriate.</h2>
    <p>For a non-technical team running a single workflow, a visual platform like Zapier or n8n is often the correct choice. The cost and lock-in concerns above do not apply meaningfully at that scale, and the platform's strength is making one-off automations accessible without engineering effort.</p>
    <p>The architectural argument for code-first applies once a team operates three or more workflows, needs to ship new automations on a regular cadence, or considers ongoing operating cost a meaningful factor. At that point, hiring a developer to build the code-first version produces compounding returns: each additional workflow adds minimal infrastructure cost, and the codebase becomes a maintainable asset rather than a recurring subscription.</p>

    <h2>The stack I default to.</h2>
    <ul class="stack-list">
      <li><strong>Code execution:</strong> Vercel serverless functions + Vercel cron</li>
      <li><strong>State:</strong> Supabase (Postgres) or Vercel KV (Redis-style)</li>
      <li><strong>AI:</strong> Anthropic API (Claude 4.7) with prompt caching from day one</li>
      <li><strong>Email:</strong> Resend (transactional) + Beehiiv (broadcast)</li>
      <li><strong>Payments:</strong> Stripe Checkout + webhook handler</li>
      <li><strong>Observability:</strong> Vercel logs + Sentry + Plausible</li>
      <li><strong>Auth:</strong> Supabase Auth (or Clerk if you want a managed surface)</li>
      <li><strong>Version control:</strong> GitHub + Vercel preview deploys</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Total platform cost for a 3-workflow business: <strong>$73 / year + API tokens</strong>. Compare against your current bill.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="manifesto-cta reveal" style="text-align: center;">
    <p style="margin: 0;">If you're currently running automations through a no-code platform and considering a migration to owned code, the free async audit at <a href="/workflow-audit/" style="color: var(--green); border-bottom: 1px dashed var(--green);">/workflow-audit/</a> returns a written assessment within 48 hours.</p>
  </div>

</section>

